preferred-citation:
  type: generic
  title: "From Monovalent Dev to Bivalent DevOps to Trivalent DevIntOps: Intelligence as the Third Continuous Loop in Software Engineering"
  authors:
    - family-names: Chertorogov
      given-names: Pavel
  year: 2026
  month: 10
  version: "1.0"
  url: "https://devintops.org/v1/"
  abstract: >-
    DevIntOps gives a software system's learned decision state a contract: what
    each lesson records, what it may change, how its effect is checked and how
    it leaves. Many agentic SDLC proposals put an AI agent into every box of the
    DevOps figure-eight. That can make each box faster, but it doesn't, by
    itself, carry experience from one task to the next. DevOps has long valued
    learning; this paper makes it an explicit, persistent and measured third
    loop next to Development and Operations. Intelligence, with the stages
    Understand, Predict, Orchestrate and Learn and with memory and judgment at
    its core, turns outcomes into lessons that change how each stage decides.
    The same pattern serves the engineering system and the decisions a product
    makes for its users. It is a step toward self-improving software systems,
    with a bound: people keep the goals, the permissions and the scoring. A
    chemistry metaphor frames the shift: monovalent Dev, bivalent DevOps,
    trivalent DevIntOps. The paper also says what result would show that the
    loop doesn't pay.
